{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Simeon and Levi are brothers</b> — he means masters of brotherhood, for their hearts grew hot on behalf of their sister. This teaches merit on their behalf, that in their zeal for brotherhood they did what they did, to say that they are not deserving of great punishment, and the sin is not worthy of forgiveness, for it is violence. But the correct interpretation in my eyes is that he said that Simeon and Levi are complete brothers, resembling one another and joined to one another in their counsel and their deeds. And I have already explained (Genesis 34:13) that Jacob was wrathful at Simeon and Levi for killing the men of the city because they committed violence, for they had not sinned against them at all and had entered into a covenant and been circumcised, and perhaps they would return to HaShem and all of them would be included among the people of Abraham's house, and among the souls they made in Haran. And it further angered him, lest they say that the matter was done by his counsel, and there would be a profanation of HaShem's name in that the prophet committed violence and plunder. And this is the meaning of <b>into their council let not my soul come</b> — an exculpation that he was not in their council when they answered with deceit, and with their assembly he was not gathered when they came upon the city and killed them. Therefore he curses their anger and their wrath. And so Onkelos translates: 'In their council my soul was not gathered to go,' etc." "And the meaning of <b>weapons of violence are their mechuroteihem</b> according to Onkelos (Onkelos on Genesis 49:5) and all the commentators (Ibn Ezra, Rashi, Radak) is: weapons of violence are their megoroteihem (their dwellings), as it says (Ezekiel 16:3), 'your mechurotayikh and your moledotayikh' (your birth and your origins), and likewise (Ezekiel 29:14), 'upon the land of mechuratam' (their birthplace). They said that its meaning is that they committed violence in the land of their sojournings. If so, the elliptical reading of the verse is: weapons of violence to them in their sojournings. And he calls it 'the land of their sojournings' because they sojourned there afterward. But according to my opinion, he says: weapons of violence are their megoroteihem — their lives, as in the language of (Genesis 47:9), 'the days of my megurai' (my sojourning); he says that the weapons of violence themselves are their megorim, for by them they live and are sustained. And similar to this is (Job 24:5), 'like the wilderness his bread to the youths.' And because of this (Genesis 49:7), he divides them in Jacob, so that they not be established as a single body, and scatters them in Israel, so that they not gather together. And so it was for them, for the inheritance of Simeon was within the children of Judah, as it is written (Joshua 19:1), 'and their inheritance was within the inheritance of the children of Judah,' and their cities were dispersed one from another throughout all the tribe of Judah, and the inheritance of Levi — cities of refuge scattered among all Israel."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {0 "The word ה' at the end of the third sentence refers to God; rendered HaShem per divine-name rules."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:47:00.702336265Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}